---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { api } from '../lib/api';
import { href } from '../lib/url';

const [cherry, meta, network] = await Promise.all([api.cherry(), api.meta(), api.network()]);

const markets = cherry?.markets ?? [];
const byNet = [...markets].sort((a, b) => b.net_usd_pct - a.net_usd_pct);
const byHeadline = [...markets].sort((a, b) => b.policy_rate - a.policy_rate);
const top = byNet[0];
const headlineLeader = byHeadline[0];
const worst = byNet[byNet.length - 1];
const fee = (cherry?.fee_bps ?? 50) / 100;

const strat = (key: string) => cherry?.strategies.find((s) => s.key === key);
const commit = strat('commit');
const chase = strat('net');
const headline = strat('nominal');
const usd = strat('usd');
const best = strat('best_single');
const signed = (n: number) => `${n > 0 ? '+' : ''}${n.toFixed(1)}%`;
const rate = (n: number) => `${n.toFixed(1).replace('.0', '')}%`;
---
<Base
  title="The highest rate is not the highest return"
  subtitle="Every number here is after the currency moved, after that country's own inflation, and after our fee. It is the only comparison worth making — and it changes the ranking."
  network={network}
  meta={meta}
>
  {!cherry ? (
    <div class="card err"><h2>Comparison unavailable</h2><p class="small">The numbers could not be loaded. Try again in a moment.</p></div>
  ) : (
    <>
      <section class="headline">
        <div class="lead">
          <p class="eyebrow">Cherry picking · last {cherry.window_days} days of real data</p>
          <h1>
            {headlineLeader && top
              ? <>{headlineLeader.country} advertises <em>{rate(headlineLeader.policy_rate)}</em>.
                A dollar there earned <em>{headlineLeader.net_usd_pct.toFixed(1)}%</em>.
                {' '}{top.country} advertises {rate(top.policy_rate)} and paid <em>{top.net_usd_pct.toFixed(1)}%</em>.</>
              : 'Measured, not advertised.'}
          </h1>
          <p class="lede">
            That is the whole idea in one line. The rate in an advertisement is paid in a currency that moves, in a
            country with its own inflation. Subtract both and the ranking is not the one you were shown — the
            country paying the least interest was the one that paid the most to a dollar saver.
          </p>
          <div class="cta-row">
            <a class="btn primary" href="#table">See the honest table</a>
            <a class="btn ghost" href={href('/borders/')}>The other half of the number</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <aside class="verdict-card">
          <span class="k">What we do with this</span>
          <p>
            We publish it, and we do not sell it. Getting your money into another country's deposit needs a bank
            account there, and the countries with the best numbers are the ones that make leaving hardest.
          </p>
          <span class="k">What you get instead</span>
          <p class="tight">
            Dollars that earn the dollar rate, in places that pass our safety check, with your money free to leave
            any time — and this table, so you can see exactly what you are not being sold.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="table" class="card table-card">
        <div class="card-head">
          <h2>Advertised rate vs. what a dollar actually earned</h2>
          <span class="small muted">one year · {cherry.start} → {cherry.end}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="scroll">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Country</th>
                <th class="right">Advertised</th>
                <th class="right">Currency vs $</th>
                <th class="right">In dollars</th>
                <th class="right">After our {fee.toFixed(2)}% fee</th>
                <th class="right">After US inflation</th>
                <th class="right">In local money</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {byNet.map((m) => (
                <tr class:list={[m.code === 'US' ? 'base' : '']}>
                  <td>
                    <span class="country">{m.country}</span>
                    <span class="small muted">{m.currency} · {m.rate_name}</span>
                  </td>
                  <td class="right num strong">{m.policy_rate.toFixed(2)}%</td>
                  <td class:list={['right', 'num', m.fx_move_pct < 0 ? 'down' : 'up']}>
                    {m.fx_move_pct > 0 ? '+' : ''}{m.fx_move_pct.toFixed(1)}%
                  </td>
                  <td class:list={['right', 'num', m.gross_usd_pct >= 0 ? 'good' : 'meh']}>{signed(m.gross_usd_pct)}</td>
                  <td class="right num strong">{signed(m.net_usd_pct)}</td>
                  <td class:list={['right', 'num muted', m.real_usd_pct >= 0 ? '' : 'meh']}>{signed(m.real_usd_pct)}</td>
                  <td class="right num muted">{signed(m.real_local_pct)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="small muted foot">
          “In dollars” is the advertised rate multiplied by the currency's actual move — not a forecast. “After our
          fee” is what you would keep if this were our product. “After US inflation” is purchasing power: the number
          that decides whether saving gained you anything. “In local money” is the advertised rate minus that
          country's own inflation, which is what it is worth to somebody who lives and spends there.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="grid g2 stack">
        <div class="card">
          <h2>So what would cherry picking have earned?</h2>
          <p class="small muted">
            We replayed the year three ways. None of them looks at a month it has not lived through yet: the rule
            decides with last month's result, which is all anyone knows.
          </p>
          <ul class="strats">
            {[
              { s: headline, tone: 'meh' },
              { s: chase, tone: 'meh' },
              { s: commit, tone: 'ok' },
              { s: best, tone: 'ghost' },
              { s: usd, tone: 'ghost' },
            ]
              .filter((x) => x.s)
              .map(({ s, tone }) => (
                <li class={tone}>
                  <div class="srow">
                    <strong>{s!.label}</strong>
                    <span class="num">{signed(s!.return_pct)}</span>
                  </div>
                  <p class="small muted">{s!.note}</p>
                </li>
              ))}
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h2>The uncomfortable result</h2>
          <p class="small">
            Our own favourite — move to whichever country actually paid the most last month — earned
            <strong> {chase ? signed(chase.return_pct) : '—'}</strong>, less than simply sitting in the country with
            the highest advertised rate ({headline ? signed(headline.return_pct) : '—'}), and far less than the
            hindsight number ({best ? signed(best.return_pct) : '—'}).
          </p>
          <p class="small">
            The reason is boring and worth saying: switching is a bet on last month repeating, and currencies do not
            repeat on a schedule. Ranking tells you where the best net return is <em>now</em>. It does not tell you
            where it will be next month.
          </p>
          <p class="small muted">
            This is why our product does not promise to chase countries. It keeps dollars in dollars, moves only when
            a better <em>safe</em> place appears, waits between moves, and lets you leave at any time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card stack callout">
        <h2>What would have to be true to sell this</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Access.</strong> A deposit in another country needs a licensed local bank or a regulated product. We cannot open one for you, and pretending otherwise is how people lose money to a spreadsheet.</li>
          <li><strong>An exit.</strong> The high-rate countries are exactly the ones that limit withdrawals when it suits them. A rate you cannot leave is not a rate.</li>
          <li><strong>Tax.</strong> Currency gains and foreign interest are taxed where you live, usually without a clean offset. Nobody's comparison table includes this line.</li>
          <li><strong>Honesty.</strong> The product people want is "the biggest number". The product that can be defended is "no currency risk and a door". We ship the second and publish the first one's real arithmetic.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="small muted">
          {cherry.note} Inflation from {cherry.registry ? 'national statistics offices' : 'public data'} for
          September 2026; exchange rates from ECB reference rates.
        </p>
      </div>
    </>
  )}
</Base>

<style>
  .headline { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.4fr 0.6fr; gap: 22px; align-items: start; margin-bottom: 18px; }
  @media (max-width: 880px) { .headline { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .eyebrow { margin: 0 0 10px; font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.09em; color: var(--brand); font-weight: 700; }
  .headline h1 { margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 31px; line-height: 1.18; }
  .headline h1 em { font-style: normal; color: var(--brand); }
  .lede { margin: 0 0 16px; color: #5D4C38; max-width: 62ch; }
  .cta-row { display: flex; gap: 10px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .btn { border-radius: 999px; padding: 11px 20px; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; }
  .btn.primary { background: var(--brand); color: #fff; box-shadow: 0 6px 16px rgba(199,72,29,.22); }
  .btn.primary:hover { background: var(--brand-ink); }
  .btn.ghost { border: 1px solid var(--line); background: #fff; color: var(--ink); }
  .btn.ghost:hover { border-color: var(--brand); color: var(--brand); }
  .verdict-card { background: #FFFCF6; border: 1px solid var(--brand); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 16px; box-shadow: 0 6px 20px rgba(199,72,29,.08); }
  .verdict-card .k { display: block; font-size: 11px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .verdict-card p { margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 13px; color: #4A3D2C; }
  .verdict-card p.tight { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .card-head { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; gap: 10px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
  .country { display: block; font-weight: 600; }
  .strong { font-weight: 600; }
  .good { color: var(--ok); }
  .meh { color: var(--brand-ink); }
  .up { color: var(--ok); }
  .down { color: var(--brand-ink); }
  tr.base { background: #FAF6ED; }
  .foot { margin: 10px 0 0; }
  .strats { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .strats li { border-bottom: 1px dashed #F3EBDB; padding: 9px 0; }
  .strats li:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .srow { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 12px; font-size: 14px; }
  .strats p { margin: 3px 0 0; }
  .strats li.ok .num { color: var(--ok); font-weight: 700; }
  .strats li.ghost { opacity: .75; }
  .stack { margin-top: 14px; }
  .callout { background: #FFFCF6; border-color: #E8DCC6; }
  .callout ol { margin: 0 0 10px 18px; padding: 0; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.7; color: #4A3D2C; }
  .err { border-color: #E7C4B6; }
</style>
